msgid "How to use block storage devices"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:5
#: e28ec0b96321449f8f82407956eb128c
msgid ""
"*This page is part of* :doc:`device handling in qubes </user/how-to-guides/"
"how-to-use-devices>` *.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:7
#: 1d1d68c0e868439987b3901782c5fbed
msgid ""
"If you don’t know what a “block device” is, just think of it as a fancy way "
"to say “something that stores data”."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:10
#: ba67c994ecf64469aab7d830216846d7
msgid "Using the Qubes Devices Widget to Attach a Drive"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:12
#: c984d60ee2f24b5e8f0ab6fa7856709c
msgid ""
"(**Note:** In the present context, the term “USB drive” denotes any `USB "
"mass storage device <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"USB_mass_storage_device_class>`__. In addition to smaller flash memory "
"sticks, this includes things like USB external hard drives.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:14
#: 00664dd331aa47098d5b503efd58fbef
msgid ""
"Qubes OS supports the ability to attach a USB drive (or just its partitions) "
"to any qube easily, no matter which qube handles the USB controller."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:21
#: d0528b8c679e4500a87eb1d9e154431e
msgid "Qubes Devices widget tray icon"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:23
#: f85825b270b34f1abb4fb95b75f3a104
msgid ""
"Attaching USB drives is integrated into the :guilabel:`Qubes Devices "
"widget`. Simply insert your USB drive and click on the widget. You will see "
"multiple entries for your USB drive; typically, ``sys-usb:sda``, ``sys-usb:"
"sda1``, and ``sys-usb:2-1`` for example. Entries starting with a number (e."
"g. here ``2-1``) are the :doc:`whole usb-device </user/how-to-guides/how-to-"
"use-usb-devices>`. Entries without a number (e.g. here ``sda``) are the "
"whole block-device. Other entries are partitions of that block-device (e.r. "
"here ``sda1``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:25
#: fac1a72395d0499d89b00898a15ae084
msgid ""
"The simplest option is to attach the entire block drive. In our example, "
"this is ``sys-usb:sda``, so hover over it. This will pop up a submenu "
"showing running VMs to which the USB drive can be connected. Click on one "
"and your USB drive will be attached!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:27
#: 8798c9711726427d924b4d8c6699b66f
msgid ""
"**Note:** attaching individual partitions (e.g. ``sys-usb:sda1``) can be "
"slightly more secure because it doesn’t force the target app qube to parse "
"the partition table. However, it often means the app qube won’t detect the "
"new partition and you will need to manually mount it inside the app qube. "
"See below for more detailed steps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:30
#: 4008e88a04584c898839035febcfb728
msgid "Block Devices in VMs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:32
#: dfe9725199a54dedba1bbf24ed356be5
msgid ""
"If not specified otherwise, block devices will show up as ``/dev/xvdi*`` in "
"a linux VM, where ``*`` may be the partition-number. If a block device isn’t "
"automatically mounted after attaching, open a terminal in the VM and execute:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:40
#: dea66d65c6c54cf0b2350b6a81cfc87d
msgid ""
"where ``xvdi2`` needs to be replaced with the partition you want to mount. "
"This will make your drive content accessible under ``~/mnt``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:42
#: 321dfa40d6694641875413aece3f5aff
msgid ""
"Beware that when you attach a whole block device, partitions can be "
"identified by their trailing integer (i.e. ``/dev/xvdi2`` for the second "
"partition, ``/dev/xvdi`` for the whole device), whereas if you attach a "
"single partition, the partition has *no trailing integer*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:44
#: efd3a9620ae04fa98b12ac7ae478f6fe
msgid ""
"If several different block-devices are attached to a single VM, the last "
"letter of the device node name is advanced through the alphabet, so after "
"``xvdi`` the next device will be named ``xvdj``, the next ``xvdk``, and so "
"on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:46
#: fe4a84587ca24eb8ae22fb2fe76ff07d
msgid ""
"To specify this device node name, you need to use the command line tool and "
"its :ref:`frontend-dev-option <user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-"
"devices:frontend-dev>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:49
#: cbd2bae7786145bb9f599848eb1d6c02
msgid "Command Line Tool Guide"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:51
#: 0f2476b69e9849808e4c512a8e1a4329
msgid ""
"The command-line tool you may use to mount whole USB drives or their "
"partitions is ``qvm-block``, a shortcut for ``qvm-device block``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:53
#: 86e9e9fb0cc84b1bbb32381feb13a4b2
msgid ""
"``qvm-block`` won’t recognise your device by any given name, but rather the "
"device-node the sourceVM assigns. So make sure you have the drive available "
"in the sourceVM, then list the available block devices (step 1.) to find the "
"corresponding device-node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-use-block-storage-devices.rst:55
#: 425e7f634e714d71a7c7dc632691f740
msgid ""
"In case of a USB-drive, make sure it’s attached to your computer. If you "
"don’t see anything that looks like your drive, run ``sudo udevadm trigger --"
"action=change`` in your USB-qube (typically ``sys-usb``)"
msgstr ""

